bunsen-exit
===========

bunsen-exit: Logout dialog box with various options, using yad as GUI.

NEWS 2025/9
The default lock screen commands are currently 'xfce4-screensave-command --lock'
and 'gtklock -d' for X11 and Wayland respectively.
These defaults might change in the future, and can be changed by developers
in bin/bl-exit, by sysadmins in the installed /etc/bunsen-exit/bl-exit.conf
or by users by copying bl-exit.conf to ~/.config/bunsen-exit/bl-exit.conf
If a daemon screenlocker is not running (ie xfce4-screenlocker or light-locker)
then xss-lock is started and runs 'bl-exit-lock'.

Bunsen-exit needs xfce4-screensaver (X11), xss-lock and gtklock (Wayland) 
for the default screen lock to work 
but if alternative lockers are used that may cease to be true.
 
Files in xfce-perchannel-xml/ are to tweak the behaviour of xfce utilities:
xfce4-desktop.xml: xfce4-desktop itself is not used, but this file sets the background wallpaper
for the screenlocker password window.
xfce4-screensaver.xml: the screensaver is disabled by default, but users
can enable it from the GUI settings. The screenlocker is enabled.
xfce4-session.xml: this sets the default screen lock command to 'bl-exit --lock'
and is needed when using xss-lock.
These files are installed to /usr/share/bunsen/skel and will be imported to 
user configs.

Files in autostart/
bl-xss-lock.desktop: launches the wrapper script bl-xss-lock, and hence xss-lock,
but only if neither xfce4-screensaver nor light-locker has been launched.
xfce4-screensaver.desktop: overrides OnlyShowIn=XFCE so it will start on BunsenLabs.

Files in bin/
bl-exit: the main script
bl-xss-lock: checks that neither xfce4-screensaver nor light-locker are running,
after waiting 1s, also kills any instances of xss-lock maybe left over from a Wayland session.

